# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation for the latest version of Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD)"
msgstr "Dokumentasyon para sa pinakabagong bersyon ng Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD)"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD)"
msgstr "Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD)"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) {#sample-based-quantum-diagonalization-sqd}"
msgstr "Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) {#sample-based-quantum-diagonalization-sqd}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This package implements sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) — a technique for finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors of quantum operators, such as a quantum system Hamiltonian [\\[1–6\\]](#references). It can target Hamiltonians expressed as linear combinations of Pauli operators or second-quantized Fermionic operators. SQD-based workflows can be run on current quantum computers and have been shown to scale to problem sizes beyond what was possible with variational methods — and even beyond the reach of exact classical diagonalization methods.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapatupad ng package na ito ang sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) — isang technique para hanapin ang mga eigenvalue at eigenvector ng mga quantum operator, tulad ng Hamiltonian ng isang quantum system [\\[1–6\\]](#references). Maaari nitong targetin ang mga Hamiltonian na ipinahayag bilang mga linear combination ng mga Pauli operator o second-quantized na mga Fermionic operator. Maaaring patakbuhin ang mga SQD-based na workflow sa mga kasalukuyang quantum computer at napatunayang nag-i-scale sa mga laki ng problema na lampas sa dating posible gamit ang mga variational na pamamaraan — at lampas pa nga sa abot ng mga eksaktong classical na paraan ng diagonalization.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "SQD-based workflows involve first preparing one or more quantum states on a quantum device and sampling from them. Then, classical distributed computing is used to process those noisy samples. This processing occurs iteratively in two steps: first, a configuration recovery step corrects noisy samples using information about the input problem; second, the Hamiltonian is projected and diagonalized in the subspace spanned by those samples. These steps are repeated self-consistently until convergence. The result is an approximated lowest eigenvalue (energy) and lowest energy eigenstate of a given Hamiltonian. SQD is robust to samples corrupted by quantum noise; in fact, as long as a useful signal can be retrieved out of the quantum computer, the outcome of SQD will be insensitive to noisy bitstrings.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga SQD-based na workflow ay unang naghahanda ng isa o higit pang quantum state sa isang quantum device at kumukuha ng sample mula sa mga ito. Pagkatapos, ginagamit ang classical distributed computing para iproseso ang mga maingay na sample na iyon. Paulit-ulit itong nangyayari sa dalawang hakbang: una, itinatama ng configuration recovery step ang mga maingay na sample gamit ang impormasyon tungkol sa input na problema; ikalawa, ipino-project at dinadiagonalize ang Hamiltonian sa subspace na binuo ng mga sample na iyon. Inuulit nang self-consistent ang mga hakbang na ito hanggang sa mag-converge. Ang resulta ay isang tinatayang pinakamababang eigenvalue (energy) at pinakamababang energy na eigenstate ng isang ibinigay na Hamiltonian. Matatag ang SQD laban sa mga sample na nasira ng quantum noise; sa katunayan, hangga't may magagamit na signal na makukuha mula sa quantum computer, hindi maaapektuhan ang resulta ng SQD ng mga maingay na bitstring.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`qiskit-addon-sqd` can be used to classically process samples drawn from a variety of quantum circuits in practice. For example:\n"
msgstr "Ang `qiskit-addon-sqd` ay magagamit para iproseso nang classical ang mga sample na kinuha mula sa iba't ibang quantum circuit sa praktika. Halimbawa:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> 1.  A variational circuit ansatz with parameters chosen such that sampling the circuit produces electronic configurations on which the target wavefunction (for example, the ground state) has significant support. This is appealing for chemistry applications where Hamiltonians can have millions of interaction terms [\\[1\\]](#references). For an example of this approach applied to chemistry using an LUCJ circuit, see the [tutorial for approximating the ground state energy of the N2 molecule](/tutorials/sample-based-quantum-diagonalization).\n"
"> 2.  A set of Krylov basis states are prepared over increasing time intervals. Assuming a good initial state and sparsity of the ground state, this approach is proven to converge efficiently. As one needs to prepare Trotterized time evolution circuits on a quantum device, this approach is best for applications to lattice models [\\[2\\]](#references) instead of complex many-body Hamiltonians like those for quantum chemistry. For an example of this approach applied to Fermionic lattice Hamiltonians, see the [tutorial for approximating the ground state energy of a simplified single-impurity Anderson model](/tutorials/sample-based-krylov-quantum-diagonalization).\n"
"> 3.  A set of Krylov basis states implemented with qDRIFT randomized compilation of the time evolution operator. This approach yields shorter-depth circuits compared to Trotter-based decompositions of the time evolution and so can be used for quantum chemistry Hamiltonians. This technique has been applied to the ground state energy of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons [\\[6\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""
"> 1.  Isang variational circuit ansatz na may mga parameter na pinili para ang pag-sample sa circuit ay makabuo ng mga electronic configuration kung saan may malaking suporta ang target na wavefunction (halimbawa, ang ground state). Kaakit-akit ito para sa mga aplikasyon sa chemistry kung saan ang mga Hamiltonian ay maaaring may milyun-milyong interaction term [\\[1\\]](#references). Para sa halimbawa ng pamamaraang ito na inilapat sa chemistry gamit ang isang LUCJ circuit, tingnan ang [tutorial para sa pagtantiya ng ground state energy ng N2 molecule](/tutorials/sample-based-quantum-diagonalization).\n"
"> 2.  Isang set ng mga Krylov basis state ang inihahanda sa pagtaas ng mga time interval. Sa pag-aakalang may magandang initial state at sparsity ng ground state, napatunayang mahusay na nagko-converge ang pamamaraang ito. Dahil kailangang maghanda ng mga Trotterized na time evolution circuit sa isang quantum device, pinakamainam ang pamamaraang ito para sa mga aplikasyon sa mga lattice model [\\[2\\]](#references) kaysa sa mga masalimuot na many-body Hamiltonian tulad ng sa quantum chemistry. Para sa halimbawa ng pamamaraang ito na inilapat sa mga Fermionic lattice Hamiltonian, tingnan ang [tutorial para sa pagtantiya ng ground state energy ng isang pinasimpleng single-impurity Anderson model](/tutorials/sample-based-krylov-quantum-diagonalization).\n"
"> 3.  Isang set ng mga Krylov basis state na ipinatupad gamit ang qDRIFT randomized compilation ng time evolution operator. Nagbibigay ang pamamaraang ito ng mga circuit na mas maikli ang depth kumpara sa mga Trotter-based na decomposition ng time evolution kaya magagamit ito para sa mga quantum chemistry Hamiltonian. Nailapat na ang technique na ito sa ground state energy ng mga polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon [\\[6\\]](#references).\n"

